export const aptitudeArticles = {
  "time-and-work": "# Time and Work\n\n## What is Time and Work?\n\nTime and Work is one of the most frequently asked topics in placement aptitude tests. The core idea is simple: **if someone can finish a job in N days, they complete 1/N of the job each day.** This one idea, combined with basic arithmetic, lets you solve every variation.\n\nThink of it this way \u2014 if you can paint a wall in 10 days, you paint 1/10 of the wall every single day. If your friend can do the same in 5 days, they paint 1/5 of the wall every day. Together, in one day, you both paint 1/10 + 1/5 = 3/10 of the wall. So it takes 10/3 \u2248 3.33 days together.\n\n---\n\n## Key Concept: Work Rate\n\n> **Work Rate = 1 / Time taken to complete the work alone**\n\nIf A finishes work in **a** days \u2192 A's work per day = **1/a**\n\nIf B finishes work in **b** days \u2192 B's work per day = **1/b**\n\nWhen both work together:\n- Combined rate per day = 1/a + 1/b\n- Time taken together = **ab / (a + b)**\n\n---\n\n## The LCM Method (Fastest Approach)\n\nInstead of working with fractions, assign the **total work = LCM of all individual times**. Then each person's rate becomes a whole number, making arithmetic much easier.\n\n**Example:** A finishes in 12 days, B in 18 days.\n- LCM(12, 18) = 36 units (total work)\n- A's rate = 36/12 = **3 units/day**\n- B's rate = 36/18 = **2 units/day**\n- Together = 5 units/day\n- Time = 36/5 = **7.2 days** \u2713\n\nUse this method whenever you have 2 or more workers \u2014 it eliminates fraction arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Basic Combined Work\n\n**Problem:** A can do a job in 15 days, B in 10 days. How long together?\n\n**Step 1:** Total work (LCM) = LCM(15, 10) = 30 units\n**Step 2:** A = 30/15 = 2 units/day; B = 30/10 = 3 units/day\n**Step 3:** Together = 5 units/day\n**Step 4:** Time = 30/5 = **6 days**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 One Person Leaves Early\n\n**Problem:** A and B work together for 4 days. Then A leaves. B finishes in 6 more days. B alone takes 12 days. How long does A take alone?\n\n**Step 1:** Let total work = 1 unit.\n**Step 2:** B's rate = 1/12 per day.\n**Step 3:** Work done by B in remaining 6 days = 6 \u00d7 1/12 = 1/2\n**Step 4:** Work done in first 4 days by A+B = 1 - 1/2 = 1/2\n**Step 5:** (A+B) rate = (1/2) / 4 = 1/8 per day\n**Step 6:** A's rate = 1/8 - 1/12 = 3/24 - 2/24 = 1/24\n**Answer:** A alone takes **24 days**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Alternating Days\n\n**Problem:** A and B work on alternate days (A on day 1, B on day 2, A on day 3...). A alone: 6 days. B alone: 8 days. When is the work done?\n\n**Step 1:** Total work = LCM(6, 8) = 24 units\n**Step 2:** A = 4 units/day, B = 3 units/day\n**Step 3:** In every 2 days (one cycle): A + B = 7 units\n**Step 4:** After 6 cycles (12 days): 42 units done. But total = 24!\nHmm \u2014 3 full cycles (6 days) = 21 units. Remaining = 3 units.\nDay 7: A works \u2192 3 units = exactly 3. **Done on day 7.**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Pipes and Cisterns\n\nThis is exactly the same concept. Inlet pipes fill (+), outlet pipes drain (\u2212).\n\n**Rules:**\n- Pipe fills tank in H hours \u2192 rate = +1/H per hour\n- Pipe drains tank in K hours \u2192 rate = \u22121/K per hour\n- Net rate = sum of all rates\n\n**Example:** Pipe A fills in 6 hours. Pipe B drains in 10 hours. Both open. Time to fill?\n- Net rate = 1/6 \u2212 1/10 = 5/30 \u2212 3/30 = 2/30 = 1/15\n- Time = **15 hours**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 5 \u2014 Men, Days, Hours (MDH Formula)\n\nWhen the number of workers, hours per day, or amount of work all change:\n\n> **M\u2081 \u00d7 D\u2081 \u00d7 H\u2081 / W\u2081 = M\u2082 \u00d7 D\u2082 \u00d7 H\u2082 / W\u2082**\n\nWhere M = men, D = days, H = hours/day, W = total work done.\n\n**Example:** 8 men working 9 hours/day finish a job in 20 days. How many men are needed to finish it in 12 days working 10 hours/day?\n- M\u2081\u00d7D\u2081\u00d7H\u2081 = 8\u00d720\u00d79 = 1440\n- 1440 = M\u2082 \u00d7 12 \u00d7 10 = 120 \u00d7 M\u2082\n- M\u2082 = 1440/120 = **12 men**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n| Mistake | What to do instead |\n|---|---|\n| Treating \"efficiency\" and \"rate\" as different things | They are the same \u2014 1/days |\n| Adding percentages instead of rates | Always add fractions (1/a + 1/b), not a+b |\n| Forgetting to subtract work when someone leaves | Track remaining work after each phase |\n| Using days directly as the rate | The rate is 1/days, not days |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Scenario | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| A and B together | ab/(a+b) days |\n| A, B, C together | 1/(1/a + 1/b + 1/c) days |\n| Efficiency ratio A:B = m:n | Time ratio = n:m |\n| Pipe fills in H, drains in K | Net: 1/H \u2212 1/K |\n| MDH formula | M\u2081D\u2081H\u2081 = M\u2082D\u2082H\u2082 (same work) |\n",
  "profit-and-loss": "# Profit and Loss\n\n## What is Profit and Loss?\n\nEvery commerce transaction results in either a profit (selling above cost) or a loss (selling below cost). Profit and Loss questions test your ability to navigate between Cost Price, Selling Price, Marked Price, and Discount \u2014 keeping track of which value is the \"base\" for the percentage.\n\nThe most important rule to memorize: **Profit% and Loss% are ALWAYS calculated on the Cost Price, never on the Selling Price** (unless the question explicitly says otherwise).\n\n---\n\n## Core Definitions\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Cost Price (CP)** | The price at which an item is purchased |\n| **Selling Price (SP)** | The price at which it is sold |\n| **Profit** | SP \u2212 CP (when SP > CP) |\n| **Loss** | CP \u2212 SP (when CP > SP) |\n| **Marked Price (MP)** | The listed/tag price before discount |\n| **Discount** | Reduction on Marked Price |\n\n---\n\n## Key Formulas\n\n**Profit%** = (Profit / CP) \u00d7 100\n\n**Loss%** = (Loss / CP) \u00d7 100\n\n**SP when Profit% = P** \u2192 SP = CP \u00d7 (1 + P/100)\n\n**SP when Loss% = L** \u2192 SP = CP \u00d7 (1 \u2212 L/100)\n\n**CP when SP and Profit% known** \u2192 CP = SP / (1 + P/100)\n\n**CP when SP and Loss% known** \u2192 CP = SP / (1 \u2212 L/100)\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Finding Profit or Loss Percentage\n\n**Example:** Bought for \u20b9400, sold for \u20b9480.\n- Profit = 480 \u2212 400 = \u20b980\n- Profit% = (80/400) \u00d7 100 = **20%**\n\n**Example:** Bought for \u20b9600, sold for \u20b9540.\n- Loss = 600 \u2212 540 = \u20b960\n- Loss% = (60/600) \u00d7 100 = **10%**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Finding SP or CP from Percentage\n\n**Example:** CP = \u20b9800, profit = 15%. Find SP.\n- SP = 800 \u00d7 (1 + 15/100) = 800 \u00d7 1.15 = **\u20b9920**\n\n**Example:** SP = \u20b9680 at a 15% loss. Find CP.\n- CP = 680 / (1 \u2212 0.15) = 680 / 0.85 = **\u20b9800**\n\n**Common mistake:** Students subtract 15% of SP instead of dividing. \u20b9680 \u00d7 0.15 \u2260 the loss on cost price.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Marked Price, Discount, and Net Profit\n\nThe retail chain works like this:\n1. Shopkeeper buys at CP\n2. Marks it up to MP (e.g. 40% above CP)\n3. Offers discount on MP\n4. Net profit = SP \u2212 CP\n\n**Example:** CP = \u20b9100. Marked up by 40% \u2192 MP = \u20b9140. Discount of 25% on MP.\n- SP = 140 \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.25) = 140 \u00d7 0.75 = \u20b9105\n- Profit = 105 \u2212 100 = \u20b95 \u2192 **Profit% = 5%**\n\n**Shortcut:** If markup = a% and discount = b%:\nNet change% = a \u2212 b \u2212 (a \u00d7 b / 100)\n= 40 \u2212 25 \u2212 (40\u00d725/100) = 15 \u2212 10 = **5%** \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Successive Discounts\n\nTwo discounts d1% and d2% applied one after the other are NOT equal to (d1+d2)%.\n\n**Equivalent single discount** = d1 + d2 \u2212 (d1 \u00d7 d2) / 100\n\n**Example:** 20% and 10% successive discounts.\n= 20 + 10 \u2212 (20\u00d710/100) = 30 \u2212 2 = **28%** (not 30%)\n\n**Proof:** On \u20b9100: After 20% \u2192 \u20b980. After 10% \u2192 \u20b972.\nDiscount = \u20b928 = 28% \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 5 \u2014 The Equal SP Trap\n\n**\"Two items sold at the same price, one at X% profit and one at X% loss.\"**\n\nThis **always** results in a net loss:\n> Net Loss% = **(X\u00b2/100)%**\n\n**Example:** Two items at \u20b91000 each \u2014 one at 20% profit, one at 20% loss.\n- CP\u2081 = 1000/1.2 = \u20b9833.33; CP\u2082 = 1000/0.8 = \u20b91250\n- Total CP = \u20b92083.33; Total SP = \u20b92000\n- Net Loss% = 20\u00b2/100 = **4%** \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 6 \u2014 Dishonest Shopkeeper\n\nShopkeeper uses a faulty weight \u2014 says it's 1000g but actually weighs only 900g.\n\n**Gain%** = (True weight \u2212 False weight) / False weight \u00d7 100\n= (1000 \u2212 900) / 900 \u00d7 100 = **11.11%**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Correct Approach |\n|---|---|\n| Profit% = (Profit/SP) \u00d7 100 | Profit% = (Profit/CP) \u00d7 100 |\n| Adding successive discounts directly | Use d1+d2\u2212(d1\u00d7d2)/100 |\n| Subtracting X% from SP to find CP | Divide SP by (1 \u00b1 X/100) |\n| Ignoring that two equal SP causes net loss | Remember the X\u00b2/100 rule |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference Table\n\n| Formula | Expression |\n|---|---|\n| Profit% | (SP\u2212CP)/CP \u00d7 100 |\n| SP from CP & P% | CP \u00d7 (1 + P/100) |\n| CP from SP & P% | SP / (1 + P/100) |\n| CP from SP & L% | SP / (1 \u2212 L/100) |\n| Equivalent discount | d1+d2\u2212(d1\u00d7d2)/100 |\n| Mark up then discount | net = a\u2212b\u2212ab/100 |\n| Same SP equal %: result | Loss = (common%)\u00b2/100 |\n",
  "speed-time-distance": "# Speed, Time and Distance\n\n## What is Speed-Time-Distance?\n\nEverything in this topic flows from one formula:\n\n> **Distance = Speed \u00d7 Time**\n\nOr rearranged: **Speed = Distance / Time** and **Time = Distance / Speed**\n\nThe key challenge isn't the formula \u2014 it's unit conversions and correctly setting up relative speeds for trains, boats, and meetings.\n\n---\n\n## Unit Conversions (Must Memorize)\n\n| Conversion | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| km/h \u2192 m/s | Multiply by **5/18** |\n| m/s \u2192 km/h | Multiply by **18/5** |\n\n**Example:** 72 km/h = 72 \u00d7 5/18 = **20 m/s**\n\n**Example:** 15 m/s = 15 \u00d7 18/5 = **54 km/h**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Basic Distance Problems\n\n**Example:** A car travels at 60 km/h for 2.5 hours. Distance?\n- D = 60 \u00d7 2.5 = **150 km**\n\n**Example:** Train covers 300 km in 4 hours. Speed?\n- S = 300/4 = **75 km/h**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Relative Speed\n\nWhen two objects move **towards each other** (opposite directions):\n> Relative speed = S\u2081 + S\u2082\n\nWhen two objects move **in the same direction**:\n> Relative speed = |S\u2081 \u2212 S\u2082|\n\n**Train crossing a pole:** Time = Length of train / Speed of train\n\n**Train crossing a platform:** Time = (Length of train + Length of platform) / Speed\n\n**Two trains crossing each other:**\nTime = (L\u2081 + L\u2082) / Relative speed\n\n**Example:** Trains of 150m and 200m travel at 60 km/h and 30 km/h towards each other.\n- Relative speed = 90 km/h = 90 \u00d7 5/18 = 25 m/s\n- Total length = 350 m\n- Time = 350/25 = **14 seconds**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Average Speed\n\n**Critical rule:** For equal distances at two different speeds u and v:\n> Average speed = **2uv / (u + v)** (harmonic mean)\n\nThis is NOT (u+v)/2. That formula only works when time is equal, not distance.\n\n**Example:** A travels from A to B at 40 km/h and returns at 60 km/h. Average speed?\n= 2 \u00d7 40 \u00d7 60 / (40 + 60) = 4800/100 = **48 km/h**\n\n**Why not 50?** Because less time is spent at 60 km/h. The slower speed \"dominates\" more.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Boats and Streams\n\n| Scenario | Speed |\n|---|---|\n| Downstream (current helps) | B + S |\n| Upstream (current resists) | B \u2212 S |\n| Speed of boat in still water | (Downstream + Upstream) / 2 |\n| Speed of stream | (Downstream \u2212 Upstream) / 2 |\n\nWhere B = boat speed in still water, S = stream speed.\n\n**Example:** Boat goes 30 km downstream in 2h and 20 km upstream in 4h.\n- Downstream speed = 15 km/h\n- Upstream speed = 5 km/h\n- Boat speed = (15+5)/2 = **10 km/h**\n- Stream speed = (15\u22125)/2 = **5 km/h**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 5 \u2014 Meeting Problems\n\n**Two people start from opposite ends of D km walking towards each other:**\n- Time to meet = D / (S\u2081 + S\u2082)\n\n**Two people start at the same point in the same direction:**\n- Time for faster to be D km ahead = D / (S\u2081 \u2212 S\u2082)\n\n**Circular track meeting:**\nIf A and B run around a circular track of length L:\n- Same direction: meet after L / |SA \u2212 SB| seconds\n- Opposite direction: meet after L / (SA + SB) seconds\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 6 \u2014 Problems with Time Difference\n\n**Example:** A is 60 km from B. P starts from A at 7 AM at 20 km/h. Q starts from B at 8 AM at 25 km/h, towards P. When do they meet?\n\nBy 8 AM, P has covered 20 km. Remaining gap = 40 km.\nFrom 8 AM: relative speed = 20+25 = 45 km/h\nTime = 40/45 = 8/9 hours \u2248 **53.3 minutes** after 8 AM.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Correct Approach |\n|---|---|\n| Using (u+v)/2 for average speed | Use 2uv/(u+v) for equal distances |\n| Forgetting train length in crossing problems | Add both lengths to distance |\n| Confusing same-direction and opposite-direction relative speed | Add for opposite, subtract for same |\n| Not converting units before calculating | Always bring to same unit first |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Scenario | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Basic | D = S \u00d7 T |\n| km/h to m/s | \u00d7 5/18 |\n| m/s to km/h | \u00d7 18/5 |\n| Train crosses platform | (Train + Platform) / Speed |\n| Two trains (opposite) | (L1+L2)/(S1+S2) |\n| Two trains (same direction) | (L1+L2)/|S1\u2212S2| |\n| Average speed (equal dist) | 2uv/(u+v) |\n| Boat downstream | B+S |\n| Boat upstream | B\u2212S |\n",
  "percentages": "# Percentages\n\n## What is a Percentage?\n\nA percentage is simply a fraction out of 100. \"25 percent\" means 25 out of every 100, or 25/100 = 0.25.\n\nPercentages are everywhere in placement papers \u2014 salary hikes, population changes, discount chains, and data interpretation. Master this topic and you'll find many other topics easier too.\n\n---\n\n## Core Conversions (Practice Until Instant)\n\n| Fraction | Percentage |\n|---|---|\n| 1/2 | 50% |\n| 1/3 | 33.33% |\n| 1/4 | 25% |\n| 1/5 | 20% |\n| 1/6 | 16.67% |\n| 1/8 | 12.5% |\n| 1/10 | 10% |\n| 1/20 | 5% |\n\nThese conversions save seconds in exams. Memorize them.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Basic Percentage Calculations\n\n**\"X% of Y\"** = (X/100) \u00d7 Y\n\n**\"What % is X of Y?\"** = (X/Y) \u00d7 100\n\n**Example:** What is 35% of 840?\n= (35/100) \u00d7 840 = 0.35 \u00d7 840 = **294**\n\n**Example:** 150 is what % of 600?\n= (150/600) \u00d7 100 = 25%\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Percentage Increase and Decrease\n\n**Increase by P%:** New value = Old \u00d7 (1 + P/100)\n\n**Decrease by P%:** New value = Old \u00d7 (1 \u2212 P/100)\n\n**Example:** A salary of \u20b925,000 increased by 12%.\n= 25000 \u00d7 1.12 = **\u20b928,000**\n\n**Example:** Price dropped by 15% to \u20b9680. Original price?\n= 680 / (1 \u2212 0.15) = 680 / 0.85 = **\u20b9800**\n\n**Critical rule:** When reversing a percentage change, DIVIDE \u2014 don't subtract the percentage from the current value.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Successive Percentage Changes\n\nTwo successive changes of a% and b% give a net change of:\n> **a + b + (a \u00d7 b / 100)%**\n\nUse negative sign for decreases.\n\n**Example:** Price first increases by 20%, then decreases by 10%.\n= 20 + (\u221210) + (20 \u00d7 (\u221210)/100)\n= 10 \u2212 2 = **+8%** (net increase of 8%)\n\n**Proof on \u20b9100:** 100 \u2192 120 \u2192 108. Net change = +8% \u2713\n\n**Special case:** Same % up then down always gives a net LOSS.\n- +20% then \u221220% = 20 + (\u221220) + (20\u00d7(\u221220)/100) = 0 \u2212 4 = **\u22124%**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Percentage Points vs Relative Percentage\n\nThese are frequently confused in data interpretation questions.\n\n**Percentage Points:** Absolute difference between two percentages.\n**Relative Percentage Change:** Change relative to the original percentage.\n\n**Example:** Pass rate was 40%, now 50%.\n- Change in percentage points = 50 \u2212 40 = **10 percentage points**\n- Relative % change = (10/40) \u00d7 100 = **25%**\n\nExaminers often ask one but students compute the other. Read carefully.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 5 \u2014 Population/Compound Growth\n\nIf a quantity grows at r% per year for n years:\n> Final = Initial \u00d7 (1 + r/100)\u207f\n\n**Example:** Town of 50,000. Grows 10% year 1, 20% year 2.\n= 50000 \u00d7 1.10 \u00d7 1.20 = 50000 \u00d7 1.32 = **66,000**\n\nNote: Each year's percentage is applied to the NEW value after the previous year's growth \u2014 not the original.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 6 \u2014 Finding the Original Value\n\nGiven the final value and the overall change, work backwards:\n\nFinal = Original \u00d7 Multiplier\n\u2192 Original = Final / Multiplier\n\n**Example:** After three 10% increases, value is \u20b913,310. Original?\n= 13310 / (1.1 \u00d7 1.1 \u00d7 1.1) = 13310 / 1.331 = **\u20b910,000**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Correct Approach |\n|---|---|\n| Reversing change by subtracting% from current | Divide by (1 \u00b1 %/100) |\n| Adding successive changes directly | Use a+b+ab/100 |\n| Confusing % points with relative % | Read the question precisely |\n| Computing P% of SP instead of CP | Profit% is always on CP |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Task | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| X% of Y | (X/100) \u00d7 Y |\n| What % is X of Y? | (X/Y) \u00d7 100 |\n| Increase by P% | \u00d7 (1 + P/100) |\n| Decrease by L% | \u00d7 (1 \u2212 L/100) |\n| Reverse an increase of P% | \u00f7 (1 + P/100) |\n| Two successive changes a%, b% | a+b+ab/100 (net) |\n| Compound growth n years at r% | \u00d7 (1 + r/100)\u207f |\n",
  "number-system": "# Number System\n\n## What is Number System?\n\nNumber System is the foundation of all quantitative aptitude. It covers divisibility, factors, HCF, LCM, remainders, and properties of digits. These questions appear in every placement test, and mastering the shortcuts can save you 30-40 seconds per question.\n\n---\n\n## Types of Numbers\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Natural | Counting numbers | 1, 2, 3, 4, ... |\n| Whole | Natural + Zero | 0, 1, 2, 3, ... |\n| Integer | Whole + Negatives | ..., \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, ... |\n| Rational | Can be expressed as p/q | 1/2, 3/4, 5, \u22122 |\n| Prime | Divisible only by 1 and itself | 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, ... |\n| Composite | Has factors other than 1 and itself | 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, ... |\n\n**Important:** 1 is neither prime nor composite. 2 is the only even prime.\n\n---\n\n## Divisibility Rules (Must Memorize)\n\n| Divisor | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | Last digit is even |\n| 3 | Sum of all digits divisible by 3 |\n| 4 | Last two digits divisible by 4 |\n| 5 | Last digit is 0 or 5 |\n| 6 | Divisible by both 2 and 3 |\n| 8 | Last three digits divisible by 8 |\n| 9 | Sum of all digits divisible by 9 |\n| 11 | (Sum of odd-position digits) \u2212 (Sum of even-position digits) = 0 or multiple of 11 |\n\n**Example for 11:** Check if 29837 is divisible by 11.\nOdd positions: 2+8+7 = 17. Even positions: 9+3 = 12. Difference = 5. Not divisible by 11.\n\n---\n\n## HCF and LCM\n\n**HCF (Highest Common Factor):** The largest number that divides all given numbers.\n\n**LCM (Lowest Common Multiple):** The smallest number divisible by all given numbers.\n\n> **HCF \u00d7 LCM = Product of the two numbers** *(only for exactly 2 numbers)*\n\n**Finding HCF (Euclidean Algorithm):**\nHCF(72, 48): 72 = 48\u00d71 + 24 \u2192 48 = 24\u00d72 + 0 \u2192 **HCF = 24**\n\n**Finding LCM:** LCM = Product / HCF = 72\u00d748/24 = **144**\n\n**When to use HCF vs LCM:**\n- *\"Largest tile to pave a floor\"* \u2192 HCF (largest common size)\n- *\"When will both events coincide again?\"* \u2192 LCM (smallest common multiple)\n- *\"Minimum rope length that can be cut into pieces of X or Y cm exactly\"* \u2192 LCM\n\n---\n\n## Remainders\n\n**Remainder theorem shortcut:**\n> Remainder of (a \u00d7 b) \u00f7 n = Remainder of (rem(a,n) \u00d7 rem(b,n)) \u00f7 n\n\n**Example:** Find remainder when 47 \u00d7 53 is divided by 5.\n- 47 mod 5 = 2; 53 mod 5 = 3\n- (2 \u00d7 3) mod 5 = 6 mod 5 = **1**\n\n**Fermat's Little Theorem (for primes):**\nIf p is prime and HCF(a, p) = 1 \u2192 a\u1d56\u207b\u00b9 mod p = 1\n\n**Example:** 2\u00b9\u2070\u2070 mod 7. Since 7 is prime: 2\u2076 mod 7 = 1. 100 = 6\u00d716 + 4.\nSo 2\u00b9\u2070\u2070 mod 7 = 2\u2074 mod 7 = 16 mod 7 = **2**.\n\n---\n\n## Unit Digits (Cyclicity)\n\nThe unit digit of powers follows a cycle:\n\n| Base unit digit | Cycle | Period |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1,1,1,1 | 1 |\n| 2 | 2,4,8,6 | 4 |\n| 3 | 3,9,7,1 | 4 |\n| 4 | 4,6,4,6 | 2 |\n| 5 | 5,5,5,5 | 1 |\n| 6 | 6,6,6,6 | 1 |\n| 7 | 7,9,3,1 | 4 |\n| 8 | 8,4,2,6 | 4 |\n| 9 | 9,1,9,1 | 2 |\n\n**How to find unit digit of a\u207f:**\n1. Find cycle period p (1, 2, or 4)\n2. Find n mod p\n3. If remainder = 0, use last position of cycle; else use that position\n\n**Example:** Unit digit of 7\u2078\u2074.\n- Cycle of 7: period 4. 84 mod 4 = 0 \u2192 use 4th position = **1**\n\n---\n\n## Number of Factors\n\nFor n = 2\u1d43 \u00d7 3\u1d47 \u00d7 5\u1d9c \u00d7 ... (prime factorization):\n> **Number of factors = (a+1)(b+1)(c+1)...**\n\n**Example:** Number of factors of 360.\n360 = 2\u00b3 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 5\u00b9\nFactors = (3+1)(2+1)(1+1) = 4 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 2 = **24 factors**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Correct |\n|---|---|\n| HCF \u00d7 LCM = product for 3 numbers | Only valid for exactly 2 numbers |\n| 1 is prime | 1 is NEITHER prime nor composite |\n| Unit digit of 9\u207f is always 9 | Cycle is 9,1 \u2014 depends on n being odd/even |\n| Using remainder of sum = sum of remainders without reduction | Always reduce mod n at the end |\n",
  "averages-and-mixtures": "# Averages and Mixtures\n\n## What is Average?\n\nAverage (Arithmetic Mean) = **Sum of all items / Number of items**\n\nIt represents a \"central\" value. Every placement exam has 2\u20135 questions on this topic.\n\n---\n\n## Core Formulas\n\n**Average** = Sum / Count\n\n**Sum** = Average \u00d7 Count\n\nIf average of n items = A, and one item x is added:\nNew average = (n\u00d7A + x) / (n+1)\n\nIf average of n items = A, and one item x is replaced by y:\nNew average = A + (y\u2212x)/n\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Finding Missing Values\n\n**Example:** Average of 5 numbers is 20. Four numbers are 18, 22, 15, 24. Find the 5th.\n- Sum = 5 \u00d7 20 = 100\n- Sum of 4 known = 79\n- 5th number = 100 \u2212 79 = **21**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Effect of Adding/Removing Items\n\n**Example:** Average of 10 numbers = 40. If one number (say 60) is removed, new average?\n- Old sum = 400. New sum = 340. New average = 340/9 = **37.78**\n\n**Shortcut:** New avg = Old avg \u2212 (removed value \u2212 old avg)/n\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Weighted Average\n\nWhen combining groups of different sizes with different averages:\n> Combined average = (n\u2081\u00d7A\u2081 + n\u2082\u00d7A\u2082) / (n\u2081 + n\u2082)\n\n**Example:** Class A: 30 students, avg 75. Class B: 20 students, avg 85.\n= (30\u00d775 + 20\u00d785) / (30+20) = (2250 + 1700) / 50 = 3950/50 = **79**\n\n**Key insight:** The combined average is closer to the average of the larger group \u2014 weighted pull.\n\n---\n\n## Alligation \u2014 The X Rule\n\nAlligation finds the mixing ratio when two things of different values/concentrations are blended.\n\n**Setup:** Two items with values v\u2081 and v\u2082 mixed to get mixture value m.\n> Ratio = (v\u2082 \u2212 m) : (m \u2212 v\u2081)\n\n**Visual (Alligation cross):**\n\n    v\u2081              v\u2082\n        \u2198          \u2197\n             m\n        \u2197          \u2198\n    (v\u2082\u2212m)      (v\u2081\u2212m)\n\nRatio of v\u2081 : v\u2082 = (v\u2082\u2212m) : (m\u2212v\u2081)\n\n**Example:** Rice at \u20b930/kg mixed with rice at \u20b950/kg to get \u20b938/kg mixture.\n- Ratio = (50\u221238) : (38\u221230) = 12 : 8 = **3:2**\n- Mix \u20b930 and \u20b950 rice in ratio 3:2.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Mixing Two Liquids\n\n**Example:** Solution A is 40% alcohol. Solution B is 70% alcohol. Mix them to get 50% alcohol. Ratio A:B?\nUsing alligation: (70\u221250) : (50\u221240) = 20:10 = **2:1**\n\n**Verification:** If we take 2 litres of A and 1 litre of B:\n- Alcohol from A = 0.4\u00d72 = 0.8L; from B = 0.7\u00d71 = 0.7L\n- Total = 1.5L alcohol in 3L mixture = 50% \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 5 \u2014 Repeated Dilution\n\nWhen V litres of pure liquid is in a vessel, and R litres is removed and replaced with water k times:\n\n> **Pure liquid remaining = V \u00d7 (1 \u2212 R/V)\u1d4f**\n\n**Example:** 40L vessel full of milk. 8L removed and replaced with water, 3 times.\n= 40 \u00d7 (1 \u2212 8/40)\u00b3 = 40 \u00d7 (0.8)\u00b3 = 40 \u00d7 0.512 = **20.48 litres**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Correct |\n|---|---|\n| Using simple average when groups differ in size | Use weighted average |\n| Getting alligation ratio backwards | (higher \u2212 mean) : (mean \u2212 lower) |\n| Forgetting the base in repeated dilution | Use V \u00d7 (1\u2212R/V)^k |\n| Adding averages directly for combined groups | Weighted: (n\u2081A\u2081+n\u2082A\u2082)/(n\u2081+n\u2082) |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Formula | Expression |\n|---|---|\n| Average | Sum / Count |\n| Replace x with y | New avg = Old avg + (y\u2212x)/n |\n| Weighted average | (n\u2081A\u2081+n\u2082A\u2082)/(n\u2081+n\u2082) |\n| Alligation ratio | (v\u2082\u2212m):(m\u2212v\u2081) |\n| Repeated dilution | V\u00d7(1\u2212R/V)^k |\n",
  "seating-arrangement": "# Seating Arrangement\n\n## What is Seating Arrangement?\n\nSeating arrangement questions give you a set of people and a set of constraints (rules about who sits where relative to whom). Your job is to place everyone in a valid arrangement that satisfies ALL constraints.\n\nThese questions test your ability to read carefully, model constraints precisely, and eliminate contradictions \u2014 not speed-solving.\n\n---\n\n## Types of Seating Arrangements\n\n| Type | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Linear (Row)** | People sit in a straight line, facing one direction |\n| **Circular** | People sit around a table; positions are relative |\n| **Double Row** | Two rows facing each other |\n| **Matrix/Grid** | Multi-row, multi-column arrangement |\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step Approach\n\n1. **Draw the template first** \u2014 draw 5 boxes for 5 people, or a circle for circular\n2. **Apply absolute constraints first** \u2014 \"A is at position 1\" or \"A sits at the northern end\"\n3. **Apply relative constraints** \u2014 \"B is 3 seats to the right of C\"\n4. **Eliminate and verify** \u2014 check that no constraint is violated\n\n---\n\n## Linear Arrangement Concepts\n\n- Positions are numbered 1 to N (left to right, unless told otherwise)\n- \"A is to the right of B\" means A's position number > B's position number\n- \"A is immediately to the right of B\" means A's position = B's position + 1\n- \"A is 3rd from the left\" = position 3\n\n**Direction trap:** If people face NORTH, then \"right\" = East (your left as you look at them on paper). Most questions assume they face you (south) unless stated.\n\n---\n\n## Circular Arrangement \u2014 Special Rules\n\nIn a circular arrangement of N people:\n- Positions are RELATIVE \u2014 there is no fixed \"1st position\"\n- Fix one person as reference (usually told: \"A sits facing north\")\n- \"Immediate left/right\" = adjacent seat in that direction\n- \"Opposite to A\" = the person sitting exactly N/2 seats away\n\n**Key:** Always fix one person and build relative to them.\n\n**Example:** 6 people A,B,C,D,E,F in a circle. A is fixed at position 12 o'clock.\n\"B is immediately to A's right\" \u2192 B is at 2 o'clock position.\n\"C is opposite A\" \u2192 C is at 6 o'clock position (3 seats away from A in either direction).\n\n---\n\n## Constraint Types\n\n| Phrasing | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| \"A and B are neighbours\" | A and B sit adjacent |\n| \"A is not next to B\" | At least one person between them |\n| \"A is between B and C\" | Arrangement is B-A-C or C-A-B |\n| \"Exactly 2 people between A and B\" | 2 seats gap |\n| \"A faces B\" (double row) | A and B are in opposite rows at same column |\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example \u2014 Linear\n\n5 people P, Q, R, S, T sit in a row.\n- T is at one of the ends\n- R is not next to T\n- Q sits between P and S\n- P is at the 2nd position from left\n\n**Step 1:** P is at position 2.\n**Step 2:** Q is between P and S. So arrangement near P is: S-Q-P or P-Q-S.\nSince P=2, either: position 1=S, 3=Q or position 3=Q, 4=S.\n**Step 3:** T is at an end (1 or 5).\nIf S=1, T must be at 5. Remaining: R at 4.\nCheck: R (pos 4) next to T (pos 5)? Yes \u2014 violates constraint!\nSo: Q=3, S=4, T=1 or 5. If T=1: R at 5. R (5) next to T (1)? No \u2014 valid! \u2713\nFinal arrangement: **T-P-Q-S-R**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Confusing \"A's left\" from different perspectives | Establish one consistent viewpoint |\n| Not checking ALL constraints after placement | Always verify every rule at the end |\n| Assuming circular has a fixed start | Fix one person as anchor, rest are relative |\n| Misinterpreting \"at least 2 between\" vs \"exactly 2\" | Read carefully |\n",
  "blood-relations": "# Blood Relations\n\n## What is Blood Relations?\n\nBlood relation questions describe a family tree through a series of statements like \"A is the father of B\" or \"B is the sister of C.\" You must determine how two people are related.\n\nThe single most effective technique: **draw the family tree on paper.** Don't try to track relationships in your head.\n\n---\n\n## Family Tree Symbols\n\nUse these while drawing:\n- **Square** = Male; **Circle** = Female\n- Horizontal line = Marriage\n- Vertical/diagonal line = Parent-Child\n- Equal sign (=) = Couple\n\n---\n\n## Generation Reference\n\n| Relation | Generation relative to you |\n|---|---|\n| Grandparents (maternal/paternal) | +2 |\n| Parents, Uncles, Aunts | +1 |\n| Self, Siblings, Cousins, Spouse | 0 |\n| Children, Nephews, Nieces | \u22121 |\n| Grandchildren | \u22122 |\n\n---\n\n## Key Relations to Memorize\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Maternal uncle | Mother's brother |\n| Paternal aunt | Father's sister |\n| Sister-in-law | Wife's/Husband's sister OR Brother's wife |\n| Nephew | Brother's/Sister's son |\n| Niece | Brother's/Sister's daughter |\n| First cousin | Uncle's/Aunt's child |\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Chain of Relations\n\n**Example:** A is the father of B. B is the sister of C. D is the husband of C. How is A related to D's children?\n\n**Draw:**\n- A \u2192 B (father-daughter)\n- B is sister of C \u2192 A is also parent of C\n- C = D's wife \u2192 A is C's father\n- D's children = A's grandchildren\n- A is their **maternal grandfather**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Pointing at a Photograph\n\nThese are the most common type in placement tests.\n\n**Approach:** Convert \"the person I'm pointing to\" into \"the person in the photo.\"\n\n**Example:** X points at Y's photo and says: \"Y is the son of my grandfather's only son.\"\n- My grandfather's only son = X's father (no siblings)\n- X's father's son = X himself OR X's brother\n- But X is pointing at Y, not himself \u2192 Y is X's **brother**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Gender Ambiguity\n\nSometimes gender is not stated. Don't assume.\n\n**Example:** \"P is the sibling of Q, and Q is the parent of R.\"\n- P could be brother or sister of Q\n- The answer would say \"Cannot be determined\" if gender of P matters\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Coded Relations\n\nSymbols replace relationship words.\n\n**Example:** A % B = A is mother of B; A $ B = A is father of B; A @ B = A is wife of B.\nWhat does P $ Q @ R mean?\n\n- P $ Q \u2192 P is father of Q\n- Q @ R \u2192 Q is wife of R\n- P is the father of R's wife \u2192 P is R's **father-in-law**\n\n**Approach:** Decode left to right, draw each relation, then state the final relationship.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Assuming gender when not stated | Only assign gender when explicitly told |\n| Confusing \"wife's brother\" with \"brother's wife\" | Brother-in-law works both ways, but note the source |\n| Working backwards mentally instead of drawing | ALWAYS draw the family tree |\n| Missing the \"only child\" clue | \"Only son\" = no brothers; answers must reflect this |\n",
  "syllogisms": "# Syllogisms\n\n## What is a Syllogism?\n\nA syllogism gives you 2 or 3 statements (premises) and asks which conclusions DEFINITELY follow. The key word is **definitely** \u2014 if a conclusion might be true but doesn't always have to be, it doesn't follow.\n\nThe tool: **Venn Diagrams.** Draw circles for each category, place them according to the statements, then check each conclusion.\n\n---\n\n## The Four Statement Types\n\n| Statement | What it Means | Venn Diagram |\n|---|---|---|\n| **All A are B** | A is completely inside B | A \u2286 B |\n| **No A are B** | A and B are completely separate | A \u2229 B = \u2205 |\n| **Some A are B** | A and B overlap (at least a little) | A \u2229 B \u2260 \u2205 |\n| **Some A are not B** | Part of A is outside B | A \u2284 B |\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step Approach\n\n1. Read all given statements\n2. Draw the most general Venn diagram (consider all possible cases)\n3. For each conclusion, check if it holds in ALL possible diagrams\n4. If it holds in every possible case \u2192 conclusion follows\n5. If there exists even one case where it fails \u2192 conclusion does NOT follow\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Universal Positive (All A are B)\n\n**Statements:** All cats are animals. All animals are mammals.\n**Diagram:** Cats \u2286 Animals \u2286 Mammals\n\n**Conclusions:**\n- All cats are mammals \u2192 \u2713 (cats inside animals, animals inside mammals)\n- Some mammals are animals \u2192 \u2713 (animals are a subset of mammals)\n- No cats are mammals \u2192 \u2717 (directly contradicts diagram)\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Universal Negative (No A are B)\n\n**Statements:** No doctor is a singer. All singers are performers.\n**Diagram:** Doctors and Singers are disjoint. Singers \u2286 Performers.\n\n**Conclusions:**\n- No doctor is a performer \u2192 \u2717 (Doctors are separate from Singers, but Doctors might independently be Performers)\n- Some performers are not doctors \u2192 \u2713 (Singers are performers, and Singers are not doctors)\n- Some performers are singers \u2192 \u2713 (since all singers are performers)\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Particular Positive (Some A are B)\n\n**Statements:** Some students are athletes. All athletes are fit.\n**Diagram:** Students and Athletes partially overlap. Athletes \u2286 Fit.\n\n**Conclusions:**\n- Some students are fit \u2192 \u2713 (the students who are athletes are definitely fit)\n- All students are fit \u2192 \u2717 (only SOME students are athletes; others may not be fit)\n- Some fit persons are students \u2192 \u2713 (same reason as first conclusion)\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 The \"Either/Or\" Case\n\nWhen neither Conclusion I nor Conclusion II independently follows, but together they cover all possible cases, the answer is \"Either I or II follows.\"\n\n**Example:** Some pens are books. No books are pencils.\n- Conclusion I: Some pens are pencils.\n- Conclusion II: No pens are pencils.\n\nNeither is definite. But exactly one of them MUST be true (pens either do or don't overlap with pencils). Answer: **Either I or II follows.**\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Correct |\n|---|---|\n| Assuming \"Some A are B\" implies \"Some A are not B\" | It doesn't necessarily \u2014 all A could be B |\n| Drawing only ONE diagram when multiple are possible | Draw all valid cases |\n| Treating \"All A are B\" as \"All B are A\" | Subset is not reversible |\n| Missing the \"Either/Or\" case | Check if conclusions are complementary |\n",
  "coding-decoding": "# Coding and Decoding\n\n## What is Coding-Decoding?\n\nA coding problem gives you one or more example pairs (word/phrase \u2192 code) and asks you to apply the same rule to a new word. The challenge is identifying the hidden rule quickly.\n\n**The approach:** Don't guess \u2014 analyze the pattern from examples methodically.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 \u2014 Identify the Transformation Type\n\nCheck in this order:\n1. **Shift** \u2014 is each letter shifted forward or backward by a fixed amount?\n2. **Reverse** \u2014 is the word reversed before/after shifting?\n3. **Mirror** \u2014 does A\u2194Z, B\u2194Y, C\u2194X (26+1 rule)?\n4. **Position** \u2014 are letters replaced by their position numbers (A=1, B=2...)?\n5. **Mixed** \u2014 alternating shift or word rearrangement?\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 1 \u2014 Letter Shift\n\nEach letter is replaced by the letter n positions ahead or behind.\n\n**Rule:** Find n from the example pair. If MANGO \u2192 PDQJR: M(13)\u2192P(16), +3. Apply +3 to all.\n\n**Example:** If CAT \u2192 FDW (+3), encode DOG.\nD\u2192G, O\u2192R, G\u2192J \u2192 **GRJ**\n\n**Tip:** If the shift isn't consistent, try reversing the word first, then shifting.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 2 \u2014 Mirror Coding\n\nA\u2194Z, B\u2194Y, C\u2194X... The mirror of letter at position p is at position 27\u2212p.\n\n| Letter | Position | Mirror (27\u2212p) |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 1 | Z (26) |\n| B | 2 | Y (25) |\n| M | 13 | N (14) |\n\n**Example:** FACE in mirror code.\nF(6)\u2192U(21), A(1)\u2192Z(26), C(3)\u2192X(24), E(5)\u2192V(22) \u2192 **UZXV**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 3 \u2014 Number Codes\n\nLetters replaced by numbers (A=1, B=2... or A=26, B=25...).\n\n**How to detect:** If the code uses numbers and A=1 gives consistent results, it's positional.\n\n**Example:** CAT = 3120 means C=3, A=1, T=20 (concatenated positions).\nThen DOG: D=4, O=15, G=7 \u2192 **4157**\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 4 \u2014 Word Rearrangement\n\nThe letters of the word are rearranged (not individually transformed).\n\n**Example:** NAILS \u2192 LIANS (sorted alphabetically? No. Reversed pairs? Check each theory.)\n\n**Approach:** Write both words side by side and check position of each letter.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern 5 \u2014 Conditional/Symbol Codes\n\nThe code system uses symbols where A%B means A is related to B in some way.\n\n**Example:** \"+\" means \"father of\"; \"\u00d7\" means \"brother of\"; \"\u2212\" means \"sister of\"\nDecode: A + B \u00d7 C.\nA is father of B. B is brother of C. \u2192 A is father of C. A is C's **father**.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Assuming same rule from previous question | Re-derive the rule fresh each time |\n| Only checking forward shift, not backward | Try both +n and \u2212n |\n| Not checking if word is reversed first | Some codes reverse before shifting |\n| Misidentifying A=26 vs A=1 code | Verify with the given example pair |\n",
  "series-and-patterns": "# Series and Patterns\n\n## What is Series and Patterns?\n\nSeries questions give you a sequence of numbers or letters and ask you to find the next term, identify the odd one out, or fill a missing term. The trick is pattern recognition \u2014 most series follow one of a small set of standard patterns.\n\n---\n\n## Number Series \u2014 Standard Types\n\n### Type 1 \u2014 Arithmetic Progression (AP)\nConstant difference between consecutive terms.\n\n**Identify:** Check differences: d = a\u2082\u2212a\u2081 = a\u2083\u2212a\u2082 = ...\n\n**Example:** 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, ___ \u2192 difference = +4 \u2192 **23**\n\n### Type 2 \u2014 Geometric Progression (GP)\nConstant ratio between consecutive terms.\n\n**Identify:** Check ratios: r = a\u2082/a\u2081 = a\u2083/a\u2082 = ...\n\n**Example:** 2, 6, 18, 54, ___ \u2192 ratio = \u00d73 \u2192 **162**\n\n### Type 3 \u2014 Difference Series\nThe differences themselves form a recognizable pattern (often AP or GP).\n\n**Example:** 1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 16, ___\nDifferences: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \u2192 next difference = 6 \u2192 **22**\n\n**Second difference series:** If first differences form an AP, it's a quadratic series.\n\n### Type 4 \u2014 Squares and Cubes\n\n**Squares:** 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36... (1\u00b2, 2\u00b2, 3\u00b2...)\n**Cubes:** 1, 8, 27, 64, 125... (1\u00b3, 2\u00b3, 3\u00b3...)\n**Square+1:** 2, 5, 10, 17, 26... (n\u00b2+1)\n**Cube\u2212n:** 0, 6, 24, 60... (n\u00b3\u2212n)\n\n### Type 5 \u2014 Mixed/Two Interleaved Series\n\nOdd-position terms form one series; even-position terms form another.\n\n**Example:** 1, 3, 2, 6, 4, 12, 8, ___, 16\n- Odd: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (\u00d72)\n- Even: 3, 6, 12, ___ (\u00d72) \u2192 **24**\n\nSo the blank = 24.\n\n---\n\n## Letter Series\n\n### Type 1 \u2014 Alphabetic Position Shift\nLetters advance by a fixed or increasing gap.\n\n**Example:** B, E, H, K, ___ \u2192 gap = +3 \u2192 **N**\n\n**Example:** A, C, F, J, O, ___ \u2192 gaps: 2,3,4,5,6 \u2192 **U** (gap = 6, O+6 = U)\n\n### Type 2 \u2014 Reverse Alphabet\nZ, Y, X... or mixed with forward letters.\n\n### Type 3 \u2014 Pair Patterns\n**Example:** AZ, BY, CX, DW, ___ \u2192 First letter: A,B,C,D,E; Second: Z,Y,X,W,V \u2192 **EV**\n\n---\n\n## Finding the Odd One Out\n\n**Strategy:** Identify what most members of the series have in common, then find which one doesn't fit.\n\n**Example:** 4, 9, 16, 25, 32, 36 \u2192 4,9,16,25,36 are perfect squares; **32** is not.\n\n**Example:** 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 15 \u2192 2,3,5,7,11 are prime; **15** (=3\u00d75) is not.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Checking only differences and missing ratio-based series | Always check both |\n| Missing interleaved patterns | Try reading every other term separately |\n| Assuming simpler pattern over complex one | Test your hypothesis with all terms |\n| Not noticing the series is squares/cubes | Compare terms to perfect squares/cubes |\n",
  "reading-comprehension": "# Reading Comprehension\n\n## What is RC?\n\nReading Comprehension passages test your ability to understand what the author **actually says** \u2014 not what you believe, not outside knowledge, not your opinion. Everything must be supported by the passage.\n\nThe golden rule: **The correct answer is always in the passage. If you can't point to the line, be skeptical.**\n\n---\n\n## The 4-Step Approach\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Active Reading (2\u20133 min)**\nRead the passage. For each paragraph, note the main idea in one phrase. Pay attention to:\n- Topic sentences (usually first)\n- Contrast words: but, however, although, despite, yet\n- Conclusion words: therefore, thus, hence\n- Tone words: adjectives describing the author's attitude\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Understand Question Type**\nEach question type requires a different strategy.\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Eliminate Wrong Options**\nRC has 4 options. Usually 2 are clearly wrong; 2 seem plausible. Compare the 2 finalists.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Go Back to Passage**\nNever answer from memory. Re-read the relevant lines.\n\n---\n\n## Question Types and How to Handle Each\n\n### Type 1 \u2014 Main Idea / Primary Purpose\n*\"The primary purpose of the passage is...\"*\n\nThe answer covers the ENTIRE passage. Eliminate options that:\n- Only describe one paragraph\n- Are too broad or too narrow\n- Contradict the author's tone\n\n### Type 2 \u2014 Factual/Direct Questions\n*\"According to the passage, X is...\"*\n\nFind the exact lines. The answer will closely paraphrase those lines. Don't introduce outside knowledge.\n\n### Type 3 \u2014 Inference Questions\n*\"It can be inferred that...\" / \"The author implies...\"*\n\nThe answer isn't stated directly but follows logically. Avoid:\n- Options that are too extreme (\"always\", \"never\", \"all\")\n- Options that require outside knowledge\n- Options that contradict the passage\n\n### Type 4 \u2014 Tone/Attitude Questions\n*\"The author's attitude toward X is best described as...\"*\n\nLook for adjectives and verbs used to describe X. Common correct tones: critical, objective, skeptical, appreciative, ambivalent, cautionary. Avoid: highly emotional extremes unless clearly stated.\n\n### Type 5 \u2014 Vocabulary in Context\n*\"As used in paragraph 2, 'acute' most nearly means...\"*\n\nSubstitute each option back into the sentence. Choose what fits the meaning in THAT context. The word may have a different meaning than its common usage.\n\n---\n\n## Trap Options to Avoid\n\n| Trap Type | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Too Extreme** | \"Always\", \"never\", \"all\", \"completely\" \u2014 rarely correct |\n| **Outside Knowledge** | True in real life but not stated in passage |\n| **Opposite** | Reverses the passage's meaning |\n| **Half Right** | First half is correct but second half contradicts |\n| **Scope Error** | Too broad or too narrow for the question |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Answering from general knowledge | Only use passage content |\n| Choosing the most detailed option | Detail \u2260 correct |\n| Not reading all options before choosing | Always read all 4 |\n| Spending too long on one question | Skip and return; don't lose time |\n",
  "sentence-correction": "# Sentence Correction\n\n## What is Sentence Correction?\n\nSentence correction questions give you a sentence with a possible error in grammar, usage, or style. Your job is to pick the version that is grammatically correct and natural-sounding.\n\nYou don't need to name the grammar rule \u2014 you need to recognize the correct structure.\n\n---\n\n## The 6 Most Common Error Types\n\n### 1. Subject-Verb Agreement\n\nThe verb must agree in number with its subject.\n\n**Singular subjects** \u2192 singular verb (is, was, has, does)\n**Plural subjects** \u2192 plural verb (are, were, have, do)\n\n**Tricky cases:**\n- \"The team **is** ready\" \u2014 collective nouns take singular (formal English)\n- \"Neither he nor they **are** right\" \u2014 verb agrees with nearer subject\n- \"Each of the students **is** required...\" \u2014 \"each\" is always singular\n\n\u274c \"The list of items are on the table.\"\n\u2713 \"The list of items **is** on the table.\" (Subject = \"list\", not \"items\")\n\n### 2. Tense Consistency\n\nDon't shift tense without reason.\n\n\u274c \"She **walked** in and **says** hello.\"\n\u2713 \"She **walked** in and **said** hello.\"\n\n**When mixing past and present is allowed:** Discussing historical facts while narrating. \"Shakespeare **wrote** Hamlet, which **remains** popular today.\"\n\n### 3. Pronoun Agreement and Reference\n\nEvery pronoun must clearly refer to one specific noun (antecedent) and match it in number and gender.\n\n**Unclear:** \"When Ravi met Ajay, **he** was nervous.\" (Who was nervous?)\n**Clear:** \"When Ravi met Ajay, **Ravi** was nervous.\"\n\n**Number:** \"Each student should bring **their** textbook\" \u2014 technically incorrect in formal English; use \"his or her\" or rewrite as \"All students should bring their textbooks.\"\n\n### 4. Parallelism\n\nItems in a list or comparison must have the same grammatical form.\n\n\u274c \"She enjoys **reading**, **to swim**, and **dance**.\"\n\u2713 \"She enjoys **reading**, **swimming**, and **dancing**.\"\n\n\u274c \"The job requires creativity, patience, and **you must be organized**.\"\n\u2713 \"The job requires creativity, patience, and **organization**.\"\n\n**Correlative pairs must be parallel:**\n\"Not only X but also Y\" \u2192 both X and Y must be same part of speech\n\u274c \"Not only did she sing but also dancing.\"\n\u2713 \"Not only did she **sing** but also **dance**.\"\n\n### 5. Modifiers (Misplaced and Dangling)\n\nA modifier must be placed next to the word it modifies.\n\n**Misplaced:** \"She almost drove her children to school every day.\" (almost modifies drove? She didn't really drive them?)\n\u2713 \"She drove her children to school almost every day.\"\n\n**Dangling:** \"Running fast, the bus was missed.\" (The bus doesn't run.)\n\u2713 \"Running fast, **she** missed the bus.\"\n\n### 6. Comparisons\n\nComparisons must be logical and grammatically matched.\n\n\u274c \"The population of India is greater than China.\" (Comparing population to country)\n\u2713 \"The population of India is greater than **that of** China.\"\n\n\u274c \"He is taller than anyone in the class.\" (Can't be taller than yourself)\n\u2713 \"He is taller than **anyone else** in the class.\"\n\n---\n\n## Elimination Strategy\n\nWhen two options seem equally correct:\n1. Check subject-verb agreement first\n2. Then check parallelism\n3. Then check modifier placement\n4. Prefer simpler, more direct phrasing\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Choosing \"formal-sounding\" over correct | Focus on grammar, not formality |\n| Missing collective noun rule | Team/class/committee = singular verb |\n| Ignoring comparative errors | Make sure you compare equivalent things |\n| Overlooking pronoun clarity | Ensure every \"it/they/he/she\" has a clear referent |\n",
  "fill-in-the-blanks": "# Fill in the Blanks\n\n## What is Fill in the Blanks?\n\nFIB questions have one or two missing words in a sentence. You must choose the word(s) that fit both the meaning and grammar of the sentence. These questions primarily test vocabulary and contextual understanding.\n\n---\n\n## 4-Step Approach\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Read the full sentence** without looking at options. What type of word is needed? Noun, adjective, verb, adverb?\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Determine the tone** of the sentence. Is the blank positive, negative, neutral?\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Look for context clues** \u2014 contrast words, cause-effect words, or descriptive phrases that hint at the blank's meaning.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Eliminate options** \u2014 cross out options with wrong tone, wrong part of speech, or illogical meaning.\n\n---\n\n## Context Clue Types\n\n### Contrast Clues\nSignal words: **although, despite, however, but, yet, on the other hand, in spite of, while**\n\nIf you see a contrast word, the blank should be OPPOSITE to the other part.\n\n**Example:** \"Although he had studied for months, his performance was ___.\"\nContrast \u2192 negative word. Answer: **disappointing** (not \"outstanding\")\n\n### Cause-Effect Clues\nSignal words: **because, therefore, so, consequently, as a result, thus**\n\nThe blank logically follows or causes the other part.\n\n**Example:** \"The bridge was poorly designed; consequently, it ___ after a minor earthquake.\"\nCause: poorly designed \u2192 Effect: collapsed. Answer: **collapsed**\n\n### Continuation Clues\nSignal words: **and, also, moreover, furthermore, additionally**\n\nThe blank should be similar in tone/meaning to what's already mentioned.\n\n**Example:** \"He was hardworking, diligent, and ___.\"\nPattern: positive work-ethic adjectives. Answer: **persistent**\n\n### Definition Clues\nThe sentence defines or explains the blank directly.\n\n**Example:** \"The speaker's ___ manner of speaking made it difficult to understand his point.\"\n\"Difficult to understand\" defines the blank. Answer: **convoluted/ambiguous**\n\n---\n\n## Double Blank Questions\n\nTwo blanks in the same sentence. Both must work together.\n\n**Strategy:**\n1. Focus on the easier blank first\n2. Eliminate options where that blank doesn't fit\n3. For remaining options, check if the second blank also fits\n\n**Example:** \"The scientist's ___ research led to a ___ that changed the field.\"\n- First blank: describes research quality \u2192 meticulous/rigorous\n- Second blank: result of good research \u2192 breakthrough\n- Best pair: **meticulous / breakthrough**\n\n**Trap pairs:** Sometimes one word fits perfectly but its paired word is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## Common Vocabulary in FIB Questions\n\n**Positive words:** meticulous, diligent, lucid, commendable, laudable, pragmatic, adept\n**Negative words:** ambiguous, abrupt, erratic, lethargic, oblivious, vacuous, perfidious\n**Neutral/contextual:** pervasive, profound, subtle, inherent, ubiquitous\n\nWhen in doubt between two positive words, choose the one that specifically matches the context.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Solution |\n|---|---|\n| Choosing a word that's correct in isolation but wrong in context | Read the full sentence with each option substituted |\n| Ignoring signal words (although/therefore) | Signal words change the entire direction of the answer |\n| Unfamiliar with vocabulary | For unknown words, eliminate by process of elimination |\n| In double blanks, ignoring one of the blanks | Both must fit; test each pair as a unit |\n"
};